{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## GATE_PEAK_RSS sample #10 — completion_verifier, from tonight's `ae96` close\n\n```json\n{\"event\": \"GATE_PEAK_RSS\", \"gate\": \"verify_completion\",\n \"ticket_id\": \"ae96-72a9-8145-4c85\",\n \"peak_rss_bytes\": 462667776, \"peak_delta_bytes\": 0,\n \"elapsed_ms\": 112617, \"timestamp\": 1788638451.668104}\n```\n\n= **441 MiB peak RSS**, 112.6 s elapsed, `peak_delta_bytes` 0.\n\nThis is the tenth `completion_verifier` sample. The nine prior samples spanned 418-440 MiB with a\nmean around 430 MiB, so 441 MiB sits **just above** the previously observed ceiling rather than\ninside it. One sample does not move a distribution, but it is worth noting that the top of the\nrange has not yet been bounded by observation — do not treat ~440 MiB as a proven maximum when\nsizing anything.\n\n`peak_delta_bytes: 0` says the gate did not grow beyond its own starting RSS during the run,\nwhich is the shape you would expect if the per-gate memory is dominated by import/startup rather\nthan by ticket-proportional work. That is a data point AGAINST gate RSS scaling with ticket size,\nthough a single close of a small bug ticket is weak evidence for that and should not be treated\nas settling AC5's RSS-vs-ticket-size question.\n\nContext: this ticket's AC5 still needs steady-state per-container RSS and the RSS-vs-ticket-size\nrelationship, both of which need on-box data rather than gate telemetry.\n\n## Live corroboration of the MCP symptom, from the same window\n\nDuring this same session pass the rebar MCP server degraded in a way that matches this ticket's\nsubject: two `comment_ticket` calls exceeded 120 s and were moved to background, and one then\nFAILED outright after 300 s with \"sent no response or progress\". All tracker writes were\ncompleted via the local `rebar` CLI instead, which succeeded immediately every time — so the\nstore and its locks were healthy (`rebar doctor`: exit 0, 0 stale locks, all six lock legs free)\nwhile the MCP transport was not. That asymmetry — CLI fast, MCP silent — narrows the fault to the\nMCP server process rather than to store contention."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788638647249330001,"uuid":"529ffc10-b083-445a-887e-1077eac5daf1"}